1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:03,791
Why is the area of a circle pi r squared?

2
00:00:04,537 --> 00:00:07,107
Cut the circle into thin rings,

3
00:00:07,454 --> 00:00:08,556
like an onion.

4
00:00:09,165 --> 00:00:12,775
Each ring is about as long
as its circumference,

5
00:00:13,195 --> 00:00:14,344
and very thin.

6
00:00:15,166 --> 00:00:17,716
Straighten each ring into a strip,

7
00:00:18,106 --> 00:00:19,236
and stack them:

8
00:00:19,826 --> 00:00:21,446
shortest on top,

9
00:00:21,576 --> 00:00:23,301
longest at the bottom.

10
00:00:24,037 --> 00:00:27,629
They pile up into something
close to a triangle.

11
00:00:29,106 --> 00:00:30,886
Now make the rings thinner.

12
00:00:31,306 --> 00:00:32,633
Smaller steps.

13
00:00:33,349 --> 00:00:35,119
As the rings get thinner,

14
00:00:35,459 --> 00:00:36,879
the steps vanish,

15
00:00:37,449 --> 00:00:39,787
and the stack becomes a triangle.

16
00:00:40,888 --> 00:00:42,788
Its height is the radius,

17
00:00:43,038 --> 00:00:43,868
r.

18
00:00:44,503 --> 00:00:46,813
Its base is the outermost ring,

19
00:00:47,013 --> 00:00:48,103
unrolled:

20
00:00:48,543 --> 00:00:49,853
the circumference,

21
00:00:49,983 --> 00:00:51,282
two pi r.

22
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:53,770
A triangle's area

23
00:00:54,130 --> 00:00:56,485
is half the base times the height.

24
00:00:57,211 --> 00:00:59,811
Half of two pi r, times r,

25
00:01:00,241 --> 00:01:01,880
is pi r squared.

26
00:01:02,895 --> 00:01:04,865
Same area as the circle:

27
00:01:05,315 --> 00:01:06,817
pi r squared.
